\section*{Claim}
For $SU(2)$, the retained single-trace loop bootstrap is closed without independent double-trace coordinates. Consequently the $N=2$ component of the plaquette certificate can be formulated as a finite affine semidefinite problem over single-trace Wilson-loop moments alone, provided the truncation retains the single traces generated by the reduction.

\section*{Premises}
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{(P1)} & \displaystyle 2w(A)w(B)=w(AB)+w(A\bar B)\quad\text{for }SU(2)\text{ loops based at the same point}.\\[0.6em]
\text{(P2)} & \text{The finite-}N\text{ normalized single-trace loop equation contains double-trace products only through overlap terms and action-loop products.}\\[0.4em]
\text{(P3)} & \text{For }SU(2)\text{ the opposite plaquette orientations have equal normalized trace in the action-loop product term.}\\[0.4em]
\text{(P4)} & \mathcal{V}_T^{(2)}\text{ retains every single-trace loop produced from the chosen loop equations after applying (P1).}
\end{array}
\]

\section*{Derivation}
Each overlap double trace in the finite-$N$ equation has the form $\langle w(A)w(B)\rangle$. By (P1),
\begin{align}
\langle w(A)w(B)\rangle
&=\frac{1}{2}\langle w(AB)+w(A\bar B)\rangle\\
&=\frac{1}{2}\langle w(AB)\rangle+\frac{1}{2}\langle w(A\bar B)\rangle.
\end{align}
Thus every overlap contribution is an affine expression in single-trace expectations. By (P3), the action-loop product difference cancels in the $SU(2)$ reduction, so it introduces no independent double-trace coordinate. Therefore every retained $SU(2)$ single-trace Schwinger--Dyson equality can be written as
\begin{align}
\alpha_0+\sum_{C\in\mathcal{V}_T^{(2)}}\alpha_C\langle w(C)\rangle=0
\end{align}
for coefficients $\alpha_0,\alpha_C$ determined by the varied loop, $\lambda$, and the overlap counts. Since (P1) is an exact trace identity, substituting it does not exclude the exact Yang--Mills assignment. Hence the resulting $SU(2)$ feasible set remains validity-preserving for the plaquette certificate.

\section*{Final Result}
\[
\boxed{SU(2):\ \text{no independent double-trace coordinates are needed after the exact trace reduction}.}
\]
\hfill$\blacksquare$

deeper_reasoning_5:
\section*{Claim}
For $SU(3)$, retaining double traces and eliminating triple traces is sufficient to close both retained single-trace and retained double-trace link-variation equations as finite affine constraints. Let
\[
\mathcal{V}_T^{(3)}=\{m_C=\langle w(C)\rangle\}\cup\{m_{A,B}=\langle w_x(A)w_y(B)\rangle\}
\]
contain all single- and double-trace coordinates generated after reduction. Then no independent coordinate of the form $\langle w(A)w(B)w(C)\rangle$ is needed.

\section*{Premises}
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{(P1)} & \text{The }SU(3)\text{ single-trace Schwinger--Dyson equation contains single-trace and double-trace expectations.}\\[0.4em]
\text{(P2)} & \text{The }SU(3)\text{ double-trace Schwinger--Dyson equation contains single-trace, double-trace, and triple-trace expectations.}\\[0.4em]
\text{(P3)} & \displaystyle \begin{aligned}[t]
w(X)w(Y)w(Z)={}&-\frac{1}{9}\bigl(w(XYZ)+w(ZYX)\bigr)\\
&+\frac{1}{3}\bigl(w(X)w(YZ)+w(Y)w(ZX)+w(Z)w(XY)\bigr)\\
&+\frac{1}{3}w(X^\dagger Y)w(X^\dagger Z)-\frac{1}{9}w(X^\dagger YX^\dagger Z)
\end{aligned}\\[1.0em]
\text{(P4)} & \mathcal{V}_T^{(3)}\text{ contains every single- and double-trace coordinate appearing on the right-hand side of (P3) after each retained variation.}
\end{array}
\]

\section*{Derivation}
By (P1), each retained single-trace equation is already an affine equality in coordinates of $\mathcal{V}_T^{(3)}$:
\begin{align}
\sum_C a_Cm_C+\sum_{A,B}b_{A,B}m_{A,B}=0.
\end{align}
For a retained double-trace variation, (P2) allows terms with three trace factors. Apply (P3) to each such product. The first and last terms on the right-hand side of (P3) are single traces, hence they are coordinates $m_C$ by (P4). The three middle products and the term $w(X^\dagger Y)w(X^\dagger Z)$ are double traces, hence they are coordinates $m_{A,B}$ by (P4). Therefore every triple-trace expectation is replaced by a linear combination of retained single- and double-trace coordinates. After replacing all such terms, the double-trace equation has the affine form
\begin{align}
\sum_C \gamma_Cm_C+\sum_{A,B}\delta_{A,B}m_{A,B}=0.
\end{align}
Because (P3) is an exact $SU(3)$ identity, the replacement preserves the exact moment assignment and introduces no uncontrolled approximation.

\section*{Final Result}
\[
\boxed{SU(3):\ \text{single traces plus double traces form a closed affine bootstrap after triple-trace reduction}.}
\]
\hfill$\blacksquare$

deeper_reasoning_6:
\section*{Claim}
A dimension-reduced path family is certificate-preserving exactly when it is closed under the operations used to generate retained constraints. In particular, the two-dimensional ladder construction is valid for vertical-edge Schwinger--Dyson variations because sewing retained paths and inserting adjacent plaquettes keep all generated loops inside the ladder family. The same closure criterion is the formal test for any plane-type path family in $d\in\{3,4\}$.

\section*{Premises}
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{(P1)} & S:=\mathbb{Z}\times\{0,1\}.\\[0.4em]
\text{(P2)} & \mathcal{P}_{\mathrm{lad}}:=\{P:V(P)\subseteq S\},\qquad \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{lad}}:=\{C:C\text{ is closed and }V(C)\subseteq S\}.\\[0.6em]
\text{(P3)} & P,Q\in\mathcal{P}_{\mathrm{lad}}\text{ with common endpoints imply }P^\dagger Q\in\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{lad}}.\\[0.4em]
\text{(P4)} & C\in\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{lad}}\text{ and a vertical-edge variation imply every inserted adjacent plaquette loop remains in }\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{lad}}.\\[0.4em]
\text{(P5)} & \text{A reduced feasible set includes only equations and positivity matrices whose generated variables stay in the selected family.}
\end{array}
\]

\section*{Derivation}
Hermitian positivity sews two retained paths with common endpoints. By (P3), this produces a loop in $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{lad}}$, so every matrix entry required by the ladder positivity constraint is represented by a retained ladder-loop coordinate. A retained Schwinger--Dyson equation inserts plaquettes adjacent to the varied edge. By (P4), vertical-edge variations of ladder loops generate only loops in $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{lad}}$, so the equation does not force an unretained off-ladder coordinate.

Thus the ladder feasible set is not obtained by falsely setting off-family generated moments to zero. It is obtained by selecting only those exact constraints whose generated moments remain in the finite retained coordinate system. Therefore the exact Yang--Mills moment assignment restricted to the ladder coordinates satisfies every retained constraint. By certificate soundness, minimizing and maximizing $x_P$ over this reduced feasible set still gives rigorous plaquette bounds.

For a plane-type family in $d\in\{3,4\}$, the same proof applies only after the analogue of (P3)--(P4) is verified for the chosen coordinate subplane and the chosen variations. Without that closure check, the reduced family is a heuristic size reduction rather than a certified truncation rule.

\section*{Final Result}
\[
\boxed{\text{path reduction is validity-preserving only through closure under sewing and retained Schwinger--Dyson generation}.}
\]
\hfill$\blacksquare$
